Modified from CBA script headers. Should be at the top of every function.

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Function: T	AG_fnc_functionName							<- Should be the internal variable that calls the function, in the casing it should be conventially called with.


Catagory: 	Uncatagorized								<- Function Catagory from https://community.bistudio.com/wiki/Category:Arma_3:_Functions

Description: A cool function that does the thing described when you call it. It might
	Even do other things which I will also include in this brief paragraph description.
	
Locailty: Local or Global								<- Does the function only change things on one machine or all

Parameters:
	-	_coolVar(dataType)				Default: ""		Controls the amount of coolness in the function.
	Include the variable name _asItAppearsInTheCode, the datatype it is or can be (String,Array). Lastly, a brief description
	explaining what it affects. A default value is not neccessary, but typically reccomeded as good practice, even for 
	mandatory variables.

Optional:
	-	_coolVarWithDefault(Integer)	Default: 0		Controls the amount of coolness, but you don't need it.
	Self explaintory mostly, for variables that may only sometimes be 

Example:
	- Always try to include atleast one example, no matter how simple its use may seem
		You can also include an explanation if its a particuarly nuanced system.

Returns:
    - Nil												<- Datatype the function WILL ALWAYS return. Must only be one type.

Notes:
	-N/A												<- Anything else that must be said can go here

Author:
    deadorbit											<- Authors, contributors, or honorable mentions
---------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

Not part of the header, but also generally good practice to define all your private
variables and parameters at the top, where they can be easily glanced at. Also important
is that if the name of the variable dosn't clearly explain its purpose, a comment should
be included around it to explain what it does.